<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tūrangawaewae</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tūrangawaewae marae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tūrangawaewae is a marae in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngaruawahia">Ngāruawāhia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, the official residence of the Māori monarch and the centre of the King movement. It was established in the 1920s on the bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikato">Waikato</a> river under the leadership of Te Puea Hērangi. Its name, meaning a place to stand, expresses the importance of home ground in Māori life, and it hosts the yearly coronation celebrations.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/turangawaewae.jpg" alt="Tūrangawaewae" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turongo_House.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In August 1921 Ngāti Tamaoho hapū, led by Princess Te Puea Hērangi, began clearing swampy land overgrown with scrub and blackberry vines, part of it recently used as a rubbish dump. Te Puea aimed to raise the mana of the Kīngitanga and its Arikinui by improving the health, housing and employment of the people and by creating a national marae at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngaruawahia">Ngāruawāhia</a> as a centre of Māori culture and politics. With the 1918 flu pandemic still fresh in local memory, she meant the Māhinārangi meeting house, built in 1929, to be a hospital where Māori could be treated in a traditional manner, but the Ministry of Health refused the permits.</p><p>The house was named after Māhinārangi, an East Coast ancestress, and her husband Tūrongo, a Tainui chief, at the request of Sir Āpirana Ngata, whose Ngāti Porou had raised thousands of pounds for it and who sent expert carvers and weavers. After a visiting prime minister called Māhinārangi a fine sitting room for King Korokī, Te Puea planned Tūrongo House, the monarch's residence, built in 1938.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Both buildings are of wood, covered in carvings painted burgundy and cream, in a blend of classical Māori and Edwardian architecture. Tūrongo House has a seven-sided corner tower representing the seven initial waka that, according to tradition, brought the Māori to Aotearoa, cladding of untreated ponga logs, and two pātaka on the roof that serve as dormer windows and hold taonga of the Kīngitanga, among them gifts from Polynesian royal houses such as that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salote-tupou-iii">Queen Salote</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The marae is the administrative headquarters of the Kīngitanga and the setting of the annual Koroneihana festivities, and its visitors have included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a> and Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a>. A renovation of both houses designed by the architects Salmon Reid in 2012, which strengthened their structures and added modern utilities, won the New Zealand Institute of Architects' Waikato-Bay of Plenty Regional Award in the Heritage Category.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tūrangawaewae” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The movement survives, with its monarch based at <b>Tūrangawaewae</b> in Ngāruawāhia.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingitanga">Kingitanga</a></li></ul></section>
